WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:07.067
In the northeast Atlantic Ocean, plankton
populations aren’t looking like they used to.

00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:12.440
These tiny marine organisms have included
an array of diatoms and dinoflagellates and

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.397
copepods, riding the currents to food and
survival.

00:00:17.397 --> 00:00:22.820
But as ocean waters have gotten warmer, the
populations of copepods and krill have declined

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:23.820
in the summer.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:30.456
And at the center of it all are tiny, photosynthetic
bacteria called picocyanobacteria who may

00:00:30.456 --> 00:00:32.941
just outlast us all.

00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:47.848
Cyanobacteria come in many different shapes
and sizes.

00:00:47.848 --> 00:00:52.530
Some are simple, some are long, and some are
just odd to look at.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:57.759
And if we needed just one word to define
picocyanobacteria, we would probably go with

00:00:57.759 --> 00:00:58.765
“small.”

00:00:58.765 --> 00:01:04.930
They’re generally 0.2 to around 2 micrometers
in length, which makes them small even by

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.670
bacterial standards.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:11.110
Piled together like this, they look like those
optical illusion paintings you’re supposed

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.540
to squint at to find the hidden image.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.540
But don’t waste your time pushing your face
closer and closer to the screen.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.220
There is no picture hiding amongst these
bacteria….

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.560
Well, or is there?

00:01:24.537 --> 00:01:30.337
There are two genera of picocyanobacteria:
Synechococcus and Prochlorococcus.

00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:33.545
And together, they kind of rule the world.

00:01:33.545 --> 00:01:38.060
At the very least, they are considered to
be the most abundant photosynthetic organisms

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.710
on our planet.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:46.303
The abundance of picocyanobacteria is matched
by just how geographically widespread they are.

00:01:46.303 --> 00:01:49.880
That's made them fascinating for scientists
who want to understand how they managed

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.680
to spread so far and how their prevalence
has shaped our planet.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.860
Assembling the history of picocyanobacteria
is a complex undertaking, but it has

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.834
some delightful theories.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:06.130
Like recently, scientists came up with one
potential explanation for how picocyanobacteria

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:11.348
were able to spread to new parts of the ocean
hundreds or millions of years ago.

00:02:11.348 --> 00:02:13.989
I'm going to bet that you can’t guess
how that happened…

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:15.920
unless you watched the video our sister

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.190
channel Eons did on this topic recently.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.170
Here’s what you need to know first.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.099
For a long time, picocyanobacteria were thought
to be autotrophic, meaning they could only

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:26.930
get their food by making it—

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.145
in their case, through photosynthesis.

00:02:29.145 --> 00:02:34.099
But it turns out, they’re mixotrophs,
able to take in elements like carbon through

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:35.180
other sources—

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:40.931
including, it seems, a material found in arthropod
exoskeletons called chitin.

00:02:40.931 --> 00:02:46.766
And scientists looking at the evolution of
picocyanobacteria came to an interesting conclusion:

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:52.852
that the picocyanobacteria might not just
have been eating chitin from ancient bug-like creatures.

00:02:52.852 --> 00:02:57.739
They might have actually been setting sail
on them, cruising around on an edible boat

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:04.630
made of discarded body parts as they began
their slow but immense takeover of the ocean…

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:12.100
an expansion that would see them become responsible
today for around 25% of all photosynthesis

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.890
in the ocean.

00:03:15.889 --> 00:03:21.440
This chitin raft hypothesis provides one possible
story for how picocyanobacteria spread around

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.550
the world.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:27.349
But prochlorococcus and synechococcus did not
accomplish what they have accomplished just

00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:28.780
by spreading.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.432
They also had to adapt.

00:03:30.432 --> 00:03:34.640
At any given point in time, their survival
is linked to a myriad of factors:

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.230
the availability of nutrients, the amount
of light they’re getting, the temperature

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:43.060
of the water, and the fact that every organism
around them is also attempting to survive

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.609
the same conditions.

00:03:44.609 --> 00:03:48.840
Adaptation isn’t a challenge unique to picocyanobacteria
after all.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.965
But they are very, very good at it.

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:55.704
They’re so good that it’s difficult to
summarize their abilities because they have

00:03:55.704 --> 00:03:59.938
adapted to so many different conditions in
so many different ways.

00:03:59.938 --> 00:04:02.567
So let’s just focus on one example.

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:07.909
Synechococcus are found in warm and cold waters
alike, which is challenging for organisms

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:13.769
like picocyanobacteria because photosynthesis
is a very temperature-sensitive process.

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:19.320
And yet synechococcus have managed to be found
in waters as cold as 2°C by altering the

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:25.410
behavior of their phycobilisome, a protein
complex that acts like an antenna to harvest

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:26.410
the sunlight.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:32.350
Part of the job of the phycobilisome is to
regulate the amount of light that the cell receives.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:36.760
It makes sure that the cell gets enough for
photosynthesis, but not so much that the organism

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:42.375
will accumulate a dangerous amount of reactive
oxygen species as byproducts.

00:04:42.375 --> 00:04:46.790
One of the way that organisms like synechococcus
limit the amount of light transmitted by the

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:52.780
phycobilisome is through a molecule called
orange carotenoid pigment, which turns excess

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.820
light into heat.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.770
The orange carotenoid protein isn’t limited
to picocyanobacteria.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.701
And in fact, not all synechococcus species
produce it.

00:05:02.701 --> 00:05:06.699
Many of the warmer-weather species don’t
even have the gene for it.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:11.630
But for those species living in colder waters,
the protein is part of their survival, allowing

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:16.265
them to adapt to a niche that would otherwise
be unwelcoming.

00:05:17.039 --> 00:05:22.303
So we saw one way that picocyanobacteria have
spread, and one way that they have adapted.

00:05:22.303 --> 00:05:25.259
But that isn't insufficient to describe
their history.

00:05:25.259 --> 00:05:27.870
Because not only do they travel and adjust.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:34.280
They also compete, putting their adaptations
to use quickly when conditions change around

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.532
them with consequences for the species that
share their home.

00:05:38.532 --> 00:05:44.569
Synechococcus can rapidly allocate resources
towards photosynthesis as waters warm, allowing

00:05:44.569 --> 00:05:47.970
them to potentially outcompete other organisms.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:53.139
So as climate change continues to see the
warming of oceans, perhaps picocyanobacteria

00:05:53.139 --> 00:05:55.580
will continue their dominance.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.610
In fact, we’re seeing it already—

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:01.830
in the krill and copepod populations that
have declined over the years in the northeast

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:07.521
Atlantic Ocean, where rising temperatures have been 
affecting the ecosystems in a number of ways…

00:06:07.521 --> 00:06:10.430
including through picocyanobacteria.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:15.784
Because along with their talent for harvesting
light, one of other advantages that picocyanobacteria

00:06:15.784 --> 00:06:17.740
is their small size.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:23.220
It makes them able to bring in so many more
nutrients relative to their tiny bodies compared

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.610
to other plankton.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:30.289
And in turn that helps them reproduce and
survive, especially as temperatures change

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.774
and affect those nutrient levels.

00:06:32.774 --> 00:06:38.290
That’s great news for picocyanobacteria,
and terrible news for copepods.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:39.290
Because those other plankton—

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:41.770
the larger ones that struggle to get as many
nutrients—

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:49.380
those are what the copepods like to eat, not picocyanobacteria,
which are just too small and lacking in nutrients.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:55.440
So as the population of non-picocyanobacteria
plankton goes down, so too does the population

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.000
of copepods and krill.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:03.389
And this in turn affects us in a lot of ways,
through the basic biogeochemical cycles that

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:08.272
serve as the foundation for our planet to
the fishing industries that provide people’s

00:07:08.272 --> 00:07:11.080
livelihoods, and food.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:17.240
And scientists predict that the global population
of synechococcus will go up around 14% by

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.110
the end of this century.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:22.133
Does that mean picocyanobacteria will win?

00:07:22.133 --> 00:07:23.305
Who knows.

00:07:23.305 --> 00:07:29.349
There are other organisms, and not to mention
entire ecosystems that surround the picocyanobacteria

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:31.739
and that shape their success.

00:07:31.739 --> 00:07:37.460
And besides, what does it mean to win,
in a world where the demands of survival are

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:39.000
always in flux?

00:07:42.567 --> 00:07:48.190
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:52.030
And remember, if you’d like to learn
about picocyanobacteria and their chitin

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:57.669
raft adventures, you can head over to youtube.com/eons
to see their episode about it!

00:07:57.669 --> 00:08:01.112
The folks on the screen right now, they are
our Patreon patrons.

00:08:01.112 --> 00:08:05.250
They’re a bunch of people who individually
could not make this show happen, but when

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:11.400
they all work together, like maybe a bunch
of bacteria in the ocean doing 25% of the

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:17.220
world's photosynthesis, they can make really
amazing and big things happen, like the existence

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:22.500
of this entire channel and its entire multi-year
history.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:23.520
Thank you so much to all of them.

